			<section class=3D"pagecontent">

				<ul>
<li><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://blog.openb=
roadcast.ch/en/radio/program/#general">General Remarks</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://blog.openb=
roadcast.ch/en/radio/program/#mon-fri">Program Monday to Friday</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://blog.openb=
roadcast.ch/en/radio/program/#sat">Program Saturday</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://blog.openb=
roadcast.ch/en/radio/program/#sun">Program Sunday</a></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"genaral">General Remarks</h2>
<p>This program layout provides the frame for the creative content of our u=
sers. It complies with the specifications in the <a href=3D"https://web.arc=
hive.org/web/20110918213307/http://www.bakom.ch/rtv_files/293_2.pdf" title=
=3D"">license application</a> and makes a distinction beween working days a=
nd the weekend. In terms of a user-generated radio the layout is valid unti=
l further developed by the community. A mode for developing it further is s=
till to be found. We are <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213=
307/http://openbroadcast.ch/en/contact" title=3D"Open Broadcast =E2=80=93 C=
ontact">open for suggestions</a>.</p>
<h3>User Generated Form of Program: Roles and Responsibilities</h3>
<ul>
<li>The producing community respects the rules of collaboration regarding t=
he <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://blog.openbr=
oadcast.ch/en/faq#requirements">functions of author and daily editor in chi=
ef.</a></li>
<li>Autors produce content: Spoken content, playlists, recordings of DJ set=
s, livesets and concerts.</li>
<li>Editors also produce content, and edit content in their function of dai=
ly editor in chief.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Program Guidelines</h3>
<ul>
<li>Open Broadcast appreciates the privilege of not having to broadcast mai=
nstream.</li>
<li>The demands for editing of the music in the individual dayparts complem=
ents the word content designated in that daypart.</li>
<li>According to the license application the overall proportionality of wor=
d/music should be 30/70.</li>
<li>A radio broadcast can last from 15 to 120 minutes. Each broadcast start=
s with a <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110918213307/http://openb=
roadcast.ch/mod/medialibrary/search/releases/name/partial/jingles/" title=
=3D"Open Broadcast =E2=80=93 Music Library">jingle</a>. About three jingles=
 should be played in one hour.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Production of Spoken Content</h3>
<p>Although the interface does not provide the function for uploading word =
content yet, creative misuse is welcome. Depending on the knowhow and techn=
ical equipment spoken content can be produced at home and uploaded into the=
 Music Library as complete broadcast or as files like music tracks.</p>
<h2>Program Concept</h2>
<h3 id=3D"mon-fri">Monday =E2=80=93 Friday</h3>
<h4>06.00-07.00 Early Bird</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> The day breaks gently with noises.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Sounds and noises of the daybreak from cities, =
forests, deserts, beaches and backyards come closer.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Not spoken. Soundscape with noises only.</li>
</ul>
<h4>07.00-09.00 Sali! (Hi!)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Waking up and leaving, in a hurry.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> A cheerful morning buzz, witty and lightfooted.=
</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Mini formats of knowledge, education, comedy, fi=
ne irony.</li>
</ul>
<h4>09.00-12.00 Mikro</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Concentration and intellectual curiosity.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Musically distinctly different: to give the spo=
ken content more room and relevance the music is exclusively instrumental, =
of all styles, including game sound and film scores but no dancefloor.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Knowledge, science and arts, exact and disciplin=
ary, in reports, interviews and speeches.</li>
</ul>
<h4>12.00-13.00 Politur</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> News different. The theme of the polyglot noon h=
our is =E2=80=9Chigher truth=E2=80=9D!</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Agit pop, polit hiphop =E2=80=93 music with soc=
io-critical or political content.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> News takeover from around the world, or polit co=
medy/satire.</li>
</ul>
<h4>13.00-16.00 Scope</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Interconnection and permeation of disciplines an=
d themes in broad focus: interdisciplinary.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Thematic programs of various styles and news. N=
o DJ sets.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Arts, knowledge, science, technology, economy an=
d everyday life.</li>
</ul>
<h4>16.00-17.00 Autour du Monde (Around the World)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> We=E2=80=99re interested in the global village.<=
/li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> The music travels along in the respective style=
s of the countries and regions around the world. This is world music in the=
 best sense.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Planet and people =E2=80=93 looks into different=
 living environments.</li>
</ul>
<h4>17.00-20.00 Brot &amp; Spiele (Bread &amp; Circuses)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Offers allure and entertain. Urban culture has l=
ost its innocence. How do fun &amp; games relate to technology, economy and=
 power?</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> House in radio edits.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> What the city has to offer =E2=80=93 culture, cu=
lture of consumption and trends =E2=80=93 in the form of event calendar, re=
port, column, disputation, review and interview</li>
</ul>
<h4>20.00-23.00 Box &amp; Live</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Artists and bohemians with their music, art, pas=
sion and expertise =E2=80=93 a box full of surprises.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Live jam, concert, label show case, DJ set, liv=
e broadcast from The Satisfactory. Alternative, experimental, special inter=
est.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Commenting: concerts, label showcases, DJ sets, =
users with friends in exchange over records.</li>
</ul>
<h4>23.00-24.00 Ozean (Ocean)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> The day ends relaxed in an ocean of sound.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Letting go of the day with mixes, where fragmen=
ts of songs, sounds and everyday noises intertwine to an =E2=80=9Cocean of =
sound=E2=80=9D (see =E2=80=9COcean of Sound=E2=80=9D by David Toop).</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> No spoken features.</li>
</ul>
<h4>24.00-06.00 Nuits Sauvages (Wild Nights)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Every night is the night of nights.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Underground, wild style, everything goes.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Underground, wild style, everything goes. Trying=
 the impossible.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"sat">Saturday</h2>
<h4>06.00-09.00 Crossfader</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Sleepy =E2=80=93 chill in and chill out.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Musically a challenge because night owls and ea=
rly birds cross paths here.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> No spoken features.</li>
</ul>
<h4>09.00-12.00 Radar</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Time to listen closely =E2=80=93 music special</=
li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Rarities, sensations, exceptions =E2=80=93 if n=
ews then under this aspect.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Commented rarities, sensations, exceptions.</li>
</ul>
<h4>12.00-17.00 Massaker (Massacre)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Soundtrack and comment on the madness of the sho=
pping rage.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Rock music from soft, to Alternative and Hardco=
re =E2=80=93 digestible because diversified.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Public space: characteristics of crowds, stress,=
 traffic, public transport =E2=80=93 in the form of ironical comment, repor=
t, interview.</li>
</ul>
<h4>17.00-21.00 Brot &amp; Spiele (Bread &amp; Circuses)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Offers allure and entertain. Urban culture has l=
ost its innocence. How do fun &amp; games relate to technology, economy and=
 power?</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> House in radio edits.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> What the city has to offer =E2=80=93 culture, cu=
lture of consumption, trends =E2=80=93 in the form of event calendar, repor=
t, column, disputation, review, interview.</li>
</ul>
<h4>21.00-02.00 Live &amp; Floor</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Fun with artists and their music, DJ sets, passi=
on and expertise.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Live and/or dance with and without guests and f=
riends.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Interviews, if any.</li>
</ul>
<h4>02.00-06.00 Tanz der Vampire</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> A night with bite and sensuality.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Dance music.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Do vampires talk?</li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"sun">Sunday</h2>
<h4>06.00-09.00 Crossfader</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Sleepy =E2=80=93 chill in and chill out.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Musically a challenge because night owls and ea=
rly birds cross paths here.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> No spoken features.</li>
</ul>
<h4>09.00-12.00 Eiertanz (Walk on Eggshells)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Tender leisure on sunday morning =E2=80=93 sitti=
ng and lying down.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> music, that breaks no breakfast eggs: organic, =
unplugged, sensuous, authentic, not synthetic. Free our folk music from its=
 negative image. Jazz.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> The music is the message.</li>
</ul>
<h4>12.00-16.00 Fiktion &amp; Wahrheit (Truth &amp; Fiction)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Believing in fiction over the fact.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Music with lyrical texts and references to lite=
rature. Songs from stage material, and dreampop: floating and bittersweet.<=
/li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Stories, poetry, slam poetry, readings, radio pl=
ays and audio books.</li>
</ul>
<h4>16.00-20.00 H=C3=B6rsturz (Pressure Cooker)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Sunday=E2=80=99s period of grace is over =E2=80=
=93 howl with laughter or being in a rage.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Alternative rock, hiphop, music to let off stea=
m.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Excerpts from your combox or texts for the Waili=
ng Wall.</li>
</ul>
<h4>20.00-24.00 Krimi (Crime)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> The tail light of the week.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Music with patina: from the Charleston of the 2=
0ies to music from early gangster- and mafia films, film noir and crime TV =
series.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> Crime in film, literature, comics and audio book=
s =E2=80=93 in the form of reviews, talk and audio series.</li>
</ul>
<h4>00.00-06.00 Creative Commons</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> A dive into the big and deep pool of productions=
 under free licenses.</li>
<li><strong>Music:</strong> Creative commons only.</li>
<li><strong>Word:</strong> The music is the message.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"info">November 2010 version</p>
